a(c)ken (attested 19th c. or earlier), also walla(c)ken and, without the velar element, wullen. All of these have the sense “to beat, brawl” besides “to toil”. Perhaps most plausibly a corruption of buerwiärken, from Middle Low German bûrwerken (“to perform obligatory services in public construction, groundworks etc.”), derived from bûr (“citizen, burgher, villager, farmer”) + werken (“to work”). Compare the byform bollwiärken (“to toil”), which was influenced by the noun at hand in English bulwark. The Westfälisches Wörterbuch also attests a form bullackern, which may reflect an intermediate step of the further development.\nOther possible connections\nThese include:\n* Low German wöhlen, German wühlen (“to dig, rummage”);\n* Low German Wull, German Wolle (“wool”), either through the noun Wullaken (“firm fabric of wool and flax”), or as in sich in die Wolle kriegen (“to brawl, fight”);\n* Low German Wallack, German Wallach (“gelding”, hence “working horse”);\n* (Low) German walken (“to waulk, full, beat”);\n* (Low) German ackern (“to toil”), from Acker (“field, agricultural land”).\nNote that even if the derivation from buer-, bollwiärken is correct, some of these influences may explain the phonetic form, especially the initial w-.",null,"haben"]],"senses":[[651132,"wullacken","verb","to toil, to work hard",0,null,null,null,"intransitive"]]},"ältlicher":{"entries":[["ältlicher",null,null,null]],"senses":[[486124,"ältlicher","adj","comparative degree of ältlich",0],[486125,"ältlicher","adj","inflection of ältlich:",1],[486126,"ältlicher","adj","strong/mixed nominative masculine singular",2],[486127,"ältlicher","adj","strong genitive/dative feminine singular",3],[486128,"ältlicher","adj","strong genitive plural",4]],"inflections":[["ältlicher","ältlich","masculine,nominative,singular,strong"]]},"abklopfet":{"inflections":[["abklopfet","abklopfen","plural,second-person,subjunctive,subjunctive-i,subordinate-clause"]]},"aufjauchzet":{"inflections":[["aufjauchzet","aufja